'''ǀXam''' (pronounced {{IPA|xam|ǀ͡kxam||audio=Pronunciation of 'ǀXam'.wav}}, {{langx|xam|ǀXam-ka ǃei|links=no}},<ref name=":1">{{Cite book |last=Blackburn |first=Julia |title=Dreaming the Karoo: a people called the /Xam |date=2022 |publisher=Jonathan Cape |isbn=978-1-78733-217-1 |location=London}}</ref> in English as {{IPAc-en|ˈ|k|ɑː|m}} {{respell|KAHM}}) is an [[extinct language]]<ref>{{Cite web |last=Boehmer |first=Elleke |last2=Staphorst |first2=Luan |date=2025-03-04 |title=Africa’s ‘sleeping’ language, {{!}}xam, has been written in stone at Oxford university |url=https://theconversation.com/africas-sleeping-language-xam-has-been-written-in-stone-at-oxford-university-250691 |access-date=2026-04-27 |website=The Conversation |language=en-US}}</ref> (or possibly cluster of languages) from South Africa formerly spoken by the ǀXam people. It is part of the ǃUi branch of the [[Tuu languages]] and closely related to the [[Endangered language|moribund]] [[Nǁng language|Nǁng]] and [[Tumʔi language|Tumʔi]] languages, the latter of which might be at least partially composed of words from ǀXam.<ref name=":02">{{Cite book |last=Sands|first=Bonny|title=Khoe and San Language Report|last2=Jones|first2=Kerry|date=2023|publisher=African Tongue|others=PanSALB|isbn=978-0-7961-4720-2|edition=1st|url=https://www.pansalb.org/wp-content/uploads/Pansalb-Khoe-and-San-Report_compressed.pdf}}</ref>{{Reference page|pages=62-69}} Apart from the possibility of it continuing as Tumʔi, the last known speakers died in the 1910s.<ref name="date">{{cite book |author-link=Anthony Traill (linguist) |last=Traill |first=Anthony |year=1995 |chapter=Interpreting ǀXam phonology: the need for typological cleansing |editor-last1=Traill |editor-first1=Anthony |editor-first2=Rainer |editor-last2=Voßen |editor-first3=Megan |editor-last3=Biesele |title=The complete linguist: papers in memory of Patrick J. Dickens |location=Köln |publisher=Rüdiger Köppe |pages=509–523}}</ref>

Much of the scholarly work on ǀXam was performed by [[Wilhelm Bleek]] and [[Lucy Lloyd]]. [[Wilhelm Bleek]], a German [[linguistics|linguist]] of the 19th century based in Cape Town, first studied a variety of ǀXam spoken at Achterveld. Later, he and Lloyd spent decades working with [[ǁkabbo]], [[Dia!kwain|Diaǃkwāin]], [[ǀAǃkunta|ǀaǃkúṅta]], [[ǃkweiten ta ǁken|ǃkweiten-ta-ǁken]], [[ǀHanǂkasso|ǀhaṅǂkassʼō]] and other speakers to document the dialects spoken by the clans in the area of the Strandberg (Clan of the 'Plain people') and Katkop mountains (Clan of the 'Grass People'), and to capture their stories and mythology.<ref>Güldemann (2011)</ref>{{Full citation needed|date=April 2026}} The surviving corpus of ǀXam comes from the stories told by and vocabulary recorded from these individuals in the Bleek and Lloyd Collection. A number of them was published in 1911 under ''[[Specimens of Bushman Folklore]]''.

== Name ==
The pipe at the beginning of the name "ǀXam" represents a [[dental click]], like the English [[interjection]] ''tsk, tsk!'' used to express pity or shame. The {{angbr|x}} denotes a [[voiceless velar fricative]] click accompaniment.

Compared to other Khoisan languages, there is little variation in rendering the name, though it is sometimes seen with the simple orthographic variant '''ǀKham''', as well as a different grammatical form, '''ǀKhuai'''.

