=== Vowels ===
The five vowel sounds are noted as {{IPA|[i e a o u]}} and are found with nasalization {{IPA|[ĩ ẽ ã õ ũ]}}, pharyngealization {{IPA|[ḭ ḛ a̰ o̰ ṵ]}}, and glottalization {{IPA|[iˀ eˀ aˀ oˀ uˀ]}}.<ref>{{Cite conference |last=Vosseler |first=Annika |title=Eine Analyse des Achterveld {{!}}Xam Korpus von W. Bleek, 1866 |conference=Linguistisches Kolloquium des Seminars für Afrikawissenschaften |location=Humboldt-Universität zu Berlin |date=26 November 2013 |url=https://www.iaaw.hu-berlin.de/de/afrika/linguistik-und-sprachen/veranstaltungen/afrikalinguistischeskolloquium/papers-wintersemester-2013-14/handout_vosseler_26_11_2013.pdf |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190809082131/https://www.iaaw.hu-berlin.de/de/afrika/linguistik-und-sprachen/veranstaltungen/afrikalinguistischeskolloquium/papers-wintersemester-2013-14/handout_vosseler_26_11_2013.pdf |archive-date=2019-08-09}}</ref>

=== Speech of mythological characters ===
{{See also|ǀKaggen|ǂKá̦gára}}
Bleek notes that particular animal figures in ǀXam mythology have distinctive speech patterns. For example, Tortoise substitutes clicks with labial non-clicks, [[Egyptian mongoose|Mongoose]] replaces clicks with ''ts, tsy, ty, dy'' etc., and Jackal makes use of a "strange" labial click, "which bears to the ordinary labial click ʘ, a relation in sound similar to that which the palatal click ǂ bears to the cerebral click ǃ". The Moon, and perhaps Hare and Anteater, even use "a most unpronounceable" click in place of all clicks save the bilabial. Other changes noted include the Blue Crane's speech, who ends the first syllable of almost every word with a /t/.<ref name="Bleek">{{cite book |last=Bleek |first=W. H. I. |year=1875 |hdl=2263/12485 |title=A brief account of Bushman folklore and other texts |location=Cape Town |publisher=Juta |hdl-access=free}}</ref>

==== "Fragment about the animal clicks and ways of speaking Bushman" ====
* The jackal has a flat lip click. 
* A kind of side click in the middle of the mouth. (referring to the jackal?)
* The moon has the joint of the tongue being turned up and back to the roof of the mouth. This click has a kind of palatal click with it.
* The lion talks with a (?) side click and a (?) guttural with it. 
* The hyena has a flat click.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://lloydbleekcollection.cs.uct.ac.za/books/BC_151_A2_1_043/A2_1_43_03356.html |title=A2_1_43_03356.JPG |website=The Digital Bleek and Lloyd |publisher=Center for Curating the Archive, University of South Africa |date=1873-10-15}}</ref>

== Motto of South Africa ==
[[File:Coat of arms of South Africa (heraldic).svg|thumb|The coat of arms of South Africa, with the motto in reconstructed ǀXam]]
ǀXam is used for the [motto on the [[coat of arms of South Africa]], which was adopted on 27 April 2000:
: ''{{lang|xam|ǃke e꞉ ǀxarra ǁke}}''
The intended meaning is ''Diverse people unite'' or, on a collective scale, ''Unity in Diversity''. The word-for-word translation is ''people who are different meet.''<ref>{{Cite book |url=https://archive.org/details/bushmandictionar00blee |title=Bushman dictionary |last=Bleek |first=Dorothea Frances |year=1956 |location=New Haven |publisher=American Oriental Society |pages=36, 363, 419, 566}}</ref> However, it is not known if that phrase would have been [[idiom]]atic in ǀXam.{{Efn|Bleek's Bushman Dictionary records {{lang|xam|ǃkʼe e꞉ ǀxarra}} with the meaning 'strangers'.}} Because it is extinct, ǀXam is not one of the [[Languages of South Africa|twelve official languages of South Africa]].

